<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">António, Prior of Crato</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1531">1531</a>–<a href="/years/1595">1595</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as António of Portugal, Prior of Crato</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">António, Prior of Crato, was an illegitimate grandson of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I of Portugal</a> and a claimant to the throne after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-king-of-portugal">Cardinal-King Henry</a> in 1580. Proclaimed king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, he was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> at Alcântara in August 1580, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> took the crown. With French and English help he held parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a> until 1583 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-prior-of-crato.jpg" alt="António, Prior of Crato" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christiaan Hagen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthony_I_of_Portugal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
